In modern MSTest (3.6+), use **constructor injection** for `TestContext` and sync setup, then `[TestInitialize]` only for async work:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class MyServiceTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private readonly MyService _service;

    public MyServiceTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
        // Sync initialization here — fields can be readonly
        _service = new MyService(new FakeRepository());
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task InitAsync()
    {
        // Async-only setup (e.g., seeding, HTTP warm-up)
        await _service.WarmUpAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public void Cleanup() => _service.Reset();

    [TestMethod]
    [Timeout(5000)]
    public async Task GetData_ReturnsExpectedResult()
    {
        var result = await _service.GetDataAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
        Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- Constructor runs first, receives `TestContext` automatically (MSTest 3.6+)
- Use the constructor for all **sync** setup — enables `readonly` fields and satisfies nullability analysis
- Use `[TestInitialize]` **only** when you need `await`
- Flow `_testContext.CancellationToken` into async calls so `[Timeout]` works cooperatively

**Execution order per test:** Constructor → `[TestInitialize]` → Test method → `[TestCleanup]` → `Dispose`
